  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAs high pressure continues to drift off the Mid-Atlantic Coast \ntoday, a cold front will approach the area as a low pressure system \nbegins to push east across the northern Great Lakes. Ahead of this \ncold front, a line of convection is expected to develop in the warm \nsector of the low over Illinois/Indiana, moving into the CWA between \n7 and 8 PM EDT. Current dewpoints over Indiana in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s mixed with the ongoing diurnally heating and theta-e \nadvection ahead of the boundary will aid in storm development. \nAdditionally, shear values of 20-30 knots should be able to aid in \nstorm maintenance as they push east. The primary concern with this \nevening's convection is gusty winds and large hail, although there \nremains a non-zero tornado chance. The tricky part about the \nforecast over the Cleveland CWA is how far east these storms will \nprogress and remain strong to severe. Given current model guidance \nplacing storms in near the I-75 corridor between 7-8PM, general \nthinking is that corridor will see the greatest potential for severe \nweather before the threat rapidly decreases further east with the \ntiming of convection becoming less diurnally favorable and \ninstability decreasing. SPC has highlight the I-75 corridor in a \nDay 1 Slight Risk (2 of 5) for the storm potential and has also \nhighlighted portions of the region upstream in a recent MCD \nhighlighting the ongoing destabilization being observed. With any \nstorms that develop, there is also a chance for locally heavy \nrainfall and due to this WPC has issued a Day 1 Marginal ERO (1 of \n4) for the northwest portion of the area. \n\nAs the front progresses east tonight, scattered showers are possible \nalong and east of I-71 but the severe threat will be minimal. This \nboundary should reach into far northeast Ohio and northwest \nPennsylvania by Thursday morning when additional thunderstorms may \noccur but should remain sub-severe given the weaker environment \nearlier in the day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHigh pressure returns to the area late Friday into Saturday and is \nexpected to persist into early next week. This should allow for a \nprolonged period of dry weather over the area in addition to the \ngradual warming of temperatures. Highs on Saturday will remain mild \nin the upper 70s to low 80s before climbing into the upper 80s to \nlow 90s for Monday and Tuesday. With dewpoints lingering in the 60s, \nit will be humid but overall heat indices shouldn't exceed the mid-\n90s. Overnight lows Saturday night will be in the 50s before they \nclimb into the mid to upper 60s by Tuesday night.",
